# Snooze plugin

Snoozes messages and returns them to the inbox at a chosen date and time.

## How it works

A snoozed message gets a `Snoozed_<unix-timestamp>` keyword and is moved to a
dedicated folder (default `Snoozed`, created on first use). Due messages are
moved back to the inbox and marked with the `Snoozed` keyword so the message
list can highlight them as returned. Opening such a message removes the marker.

The plugin stores the folder choice in the user plugin settings under
`SnoozeFolder`, so every account can use its own folder.

The snooze folder is shown in the system folder section at the top of the
folder list with a 💤 icon and is hidden from the normal folder list.

The mail server must support custom IMAP keywords (`PERMANENTFLAGS` containing
`\*` or the keyword itself), otherwise snoozing is rejected with an error.

## Installation

`plugins/snooze` in this repository is only the plugin source. SnappyMail
loads plugins from its runtime plugin folder `APP_PLUGINS_PATH`, which is
`<data>/_data_/_default_/plugins/` for a normal installation. With Docker the
path is `/var/lib/snappymail/_data_/_default_/plugins/`.

1. Copy the folder into the runtime plugin folder:

   ```sh
   cp -a plugins/snooze "<data>/_data_/_default_/plugins/snooze"
   ```

2. Enable plugin support and the plugin in
   `<data>/_data_/_default_/configs/application.ini`:

   ```ini
   [plugins]
   enable = 1
   enabled_list = "snooze"
   ```

   Alternatively use *Admin → Packages*: enable plugin support, then enable
   *Snooze* in the *Installed packages* list.

3. Make sure the web server user can read the copied files.

The *available for installation* list is loaded from the remote SnappyMail
package repository. A locally developed plugin never appears there; after
copying it is listed as an installed package (without a remote version).

## Usage

1. Open a message and click the 💤 button in the toolbar.
2. Choose a quick option or a date and time and confirm.
3. The folder can be changed in *Settings → Folders → System folders*.

Due messages are returned when the message list is loaded and every minute while
the webmail stays open.
